# A MARATHON walk, for defects that only appear after a long session.
#
#   tools/headless.sh tools/keys/marathon.keys 1
#
# Why this exists, and why explore.keys is not enough for it. inc-2k3 is
# Brian's report that the game gets slower the longer it runs, until a run
# takes long enough to crash, and that killing a group of monsters gave the
# speed back. Nothing about that shape can be seen in a session of a few
# hundred turns: explore.keys spends 1259 keystrokes and dive.keys 886, and
# the slowdown he describes builds over thousands. (Those two counts read
# "about 500" and "about 400" until 2026-08-17; both were wrong. Recount with
# the expansion rule below whenever a walk file changes.)
#
# So this is the same undirected walk, held for roughly 10500 keystrokes. It
# still walks into rock, which still spends a turn, so monsters still move and
# their AI still runs. It still takes the stairs, so levels keep being made.
# ESC still appears regularly, so a command that unexpectedly opens a menu
# cannot swallow the rest of the file.
#
# EVERY 'g' IS FOLLOWED BY 'd', AND MUST STAY THAT WAY. Added 2026-08-22.
#
# 'g' picks an item up into the In-the-Air slot. When the pack then refuses it
# -- "The medium backpack can't hold that much weight" -- the item stays in
# that slot, and Inventory Mode will not close while it is occupied:
# src/Managers.cpp:738-741 refuses both ENTER and ESC with "You cannot leave
# Inventory Mode with an item in your In-the-Air slot." This file's ESCs were
# its whole defence against a command that opens a menu, and they cannot
# answer this one. 'd' empties the slot (src/Managers.cpp:774-796), so the
# next ESC works.
#
# WHAT IT COST BEFORE THE FIX, measured with the game-time report that
# tools/headless.sh now prints:
#
#   seed   turns played   stalled from key   keys wasted   share of file
#      1          8,363              1,251         9,370            89%
#      2         47,719              2,329         8,292            79%
#      3         47,181              2,329         8,292            79%
#      7        151,157              4,562         6,059            58%
#     11         21,716              1,251         9,370            89%
#
# Every one of those runs reported "ended: cleanly", "death: none",
# "stuck-prompt: none", "errors: none". The file built to reproduce inc-2k3
# was spending most of its budget parked in a menu, and nothing said so.
#
# After the fix, seeds 1, 2, 7 and 11 play about 250,000 turns each with no
# stalled interval. Seed 3 still stalls, for a DIFFERENT reason that is not a
# key-script fault -- see inc-2k3 -- so do not read a green seed 1 as proof
# that the walk is healthy. Read the "game time:" line of each run.
#
# THE DEFAULT KEY BUDGET IS ENOUGH. THIS FILE DOES NOT NEED A RAISED CAP.
#
# This header used to read: "The default key budget is 20000 (DEFAULT_MAX_KEYS
# in src/Wposix.cpp), which this exceeds on purpose. Set INCURSION_MAX_KEYS
# above it or the session stops early and exits 3." That was wrong from the
# day the file was written, and it contradicted the 10500 figure eight lines
# above it. Corrected 2026-08-17.
#
# The arithmetic. DEFAULT_MAX_KEYS is 20000 (src/Wposix.cpp:78) and has been
# since this harness was added (commit 058ba87). The budget counts keys READ
# (keysRead, src/Wposix.cpp:1189), one per GetChar call. Expanding every
# `TOKEN*N` in this file gives 10500 keystrokes, and the included
# chargen.keys gives 111, so a session reads at most 10611. It runs out of
# SCRIPT long before it runs out of BUDGET, and exits 0, not 3.
#
# Setting INCURSION_MAX_KEYS higher is harmless and buys nothing. Raise it
# only if this file is regenerated longer than 20000 expanded keystrokes.
# Count them, do not guess:
#   python3 -c "import re,sys; print(sum(int(m.group(2)) if (m:=re.match(r'^(.+?)\*(\d+)$',t)) else 1 for l in open(sys.argv[1]) if not l.lstrip().startswith('@') for t in l.split('#')[0].split() if not t.startswith('@')))" tools/keys/marathon.keys
#
# Generated with a seed of 20260815, 380 lines of 10 tokens. NOT regenerable:
# the generator is lost.
#
# This header used to say "the generator is quoted in the commit that added
# this file". It is not. The adding commit is f76271f, its body quotes no
# generator, and no commit in the history does (checked 2026-08-17 with
# git log --format=%B). Note that f76271f is also where the false key-budget
# claim above came from, so this file inherited two wrong statements from one
# commit message. Tracked as inc-buq.

